Q2: Can the site work offline?
Yes — with a PWA (Progressive Web App) Service Worker.

Your stack (Astro + Vite) supports this cleanly via @vite-pwa/astro plugin.

What it does:

Caches pages, JS, CSS, fonts on first visit
Next visits load from cache instantly — even with no internet
Shows a fallback page when fully offline
How it works with your site specifically:

Static tool pages (all your calculator/converter tools) → fully cacheable → work offline
Dynamic things like search (/api/search.json) → can also be cached on first fetch
Form submissions (Feature Request) → need internet, but you can show a nice offline message
Trade-off: Adds a service worker (~2KB) and a manifest file. The @vite-pwa/astro plugin handles everything automatically including cache invalidation on deploy.

One-liner to add: npx astro add @vite-pwa/astro

Q3: Can this site become a mobile app for Play Store / App Store?
Two routes — you don't need to rewrite anything:

Route A: PWA → App Store (Easiest, same codebase)
Since your site is Astro (static output), it can be a PWA. A PWA can be:

Published to Play Store via PWA Builder or Bubblewrap — wraps your PWA in a TWA (Trusted Web Activity). Google officially supports this. Zero new code.
Published to App Store via PWA Builder as well, though Apple has stricter rules — needs offline support + specific manifest fields.
This is the recommended path for your case because:

Same codebase, zero duplication
All your tools work as-is
Offline support (from Q2) makes this even better
Route B: Capacitor (Native wrapper, 2–3 days effort)
Capacitor wraps your existing web app in a native shell and produces real .apk / .ipa files. Gives access to native APIs (camera, GPS, push notifications). Your tools don't need any of that, so Route A is better.

Route C: React Native / Flutter (Scratch — not recommended)
Complete rewrite, months of work, duplicate maintenance. Not justified for a tools site.